The xT-GK feature in silly_kicks/tracking/_xt_gk.py implements an analytical
Expected-Threat extension for goalkeeper distributions:

- Eyestone, J. (2025). "Expected Threat for Goalkeepers (xT-GK)." Winner,
  Pitch to the Pros 1.
  (GK-distribution value: pressure-escape, risk-adjusted, defensive-zone
  terms; team-philosophy parameter presets)

- Singh, K. (2018). "Introducing Expected Threat (xT)." karun.in/blog/
  expected-threat
  (baseline grid the GK-specific terms compose over)

The functional forms are the silly-kicks formulation of Eyestone's xT-GK (the
source deck specifies components and parameter ranges, not closed-form
equations). Contributed publicly with attribution by Jeffrey Eyestone's
explicit permission (email 2026-06-06; formula choice + preset publication
confirmed 2026-06-08; PEV/DZV fidelity formulas confirmed 2026-06-27). The
pressure-escape (PEV) and defensive-zone (DZV) terms operate on the GK-revalued
surface V_GK = xT * phi(z,d), where phi(z,d) = alpha*(1 - d/D_max)^(-beta) for d
below the defensive-third threshold (else 1); alpha and beta are canonical
(Eyestone 2026-06-27). DZV is the published revaluation multiplier's increment on
the origin possession value.

xT-GK's risk-adjusted term (RAV) draws its pass-completion probability from a
fitted GK-distribution completion model (silly_kicks/tracking/_gk_completion.py,
GkCompletionModel) and its missing goal-kick coordinates from a scoped geometry
resolver (silly_kicks/tracking/_gk_geometry.py, resolve_gk_geometry). These are
standard internal engineering (logistic regression + a conditional coordinate
prior); they implement no third-party published methodology and require no
additional attribution beyond the xT-GK and xT references above.

The Ward-clustering line-breaking detection in
silly_kicks/tracking/_line_breaking.py (PR-S33, TF-32) implements the
methodology described in:

- Karakus, O., & Arkadas, H. (2025). "Through the Gaps: Uncovering Tactical
  Line-Breaking Passes with Clustering." arXiv:2506.06666. ECML/PKDD MLSA 2025.
  (1D Ward hierarchical clustering on x-coordinates for defensive line
