- Goes, F. R., Kempe, M., Meerhoff, L. A., & Lemmink, K. A. P. M. (2019).
  "Not Every Pass Can Be an Assist: A Data-Driven Model to Measure Pass
  Effectiveness in Professional Soccer Matches." Big Data, 7(1).
  doi:10.1089/big.2018.0067.
  (Longitudinal outplayed-defender formalization: the start_x < d_x <= end_x
  inequality -- identical to TF-45's Line Bypass Score, whose far-touchline
  1-D caveat is inherited canon.)

- Varadharajan, S. K. "football-packing" (open-source library,
  github.com/samirak93/football-packing).
  (The net-packing direction multipliers: +1 forward / +0.5 sideways / -1
  backward over the traversed x-interval.)

Practitioner rules (the goal-threat last-N back-line restriction, secured
reception, and the reported per-match anchors) follow the Modern Soccer Coach
"Packing Data" lesson and the Twelve/Soccermatics Pro course materials; see
the per-feature docstrings.

The off-ball run valuation in silly_kicks/tracking/_run_values.py
(PR-S119, TF-35) implements methodologies described in:

- Sumpter, D., et al. Soccermatics Pro (Twelve Football), module 16.2:
  "Valuing off-ball runs." A PRACTITIONER anchor, stated as such -- the course
  frames a run's worth as the threat of the space the runner comes to control,
  which is the shape this module implements (pitch control x threat, taken as a
  maximum over the region the runner owns). It is not a peer-reviewed
  specification and no numeric result is reproduced from it.

- Esposito, U., et al. (2026). "Off-ball movement analysis in elite football."
  International Journal of Sports Science & Coaching.
  doi:10.1177/17479541261427153.
  (FRAMING ONLY: the target-vs-disruptive distinction between the run that
  receives the ball and the runs that pull defenders elsewhere. No estimator,
  threshold, or numeric result is taken from this paper.)

- TF-51 per-event defensive credit/debit family
  (silly_kicks/tracking/defensive_credit/). Sumpter, D., et al. Soccermatics Pro
  (Twelve Football), module 16.3: the coach-consulted per-event defensive-credit
  rules (proximity-gated signed credit to defenders, sized by shot xG or the
  attacker's threat at a turnover) -- a PRACTITIONER anchor, not a peer-reviewed
  specification; no numeric result is reproduced from it. The xT(origin) turnover
  sizing follows the published, validated precedent Bischofberger, J., Bauer, P.,
  & Baca, A. (2026), "Blame is easier than praise" (arXiv:2606.19931; code
  github.com/jonas-bischofberger/defensive-network), which derives xDT = -dxT ->
  +xT(origin) for a failed pass and validates the resulting fault/contribution
  metrics against market value + FIFA defensive-awareness ratings (consistent with
  the existing TF-28 accessible-space/DAS citation from the same group). An OPT-IN
  reverse-xT "position won" pressing lens (DefensiveCreditParams(pressing_lens=True))
  sizes the same turnover rules by xT at the 180-degree-reflected point instead --
  rewarding regains near the opponent goal; it DIVERGES from the validated xT(origin)
  standard above and UNDER-VALUES last-ditch defending, so it is off by default. MSC-corpus
  vocabulary (docstring-grade, not reproduced numerics): the Tigres Femenil
  "bravery" (% of opponent final actions blocked) metric and the RB Salzburg
  4-action pressing taxonomy. The TF-51 v2 pressure-commitment cue (whether a
  pressing defender COMMITS/drives-in vs CONTAINS/jockeys, measured as the slope
  of the defender's closing-speed) is a PRACTITIONER concept (PSG / Luis Enrique
  pressing style; Sumpter coaching literature), attributed as such -- no numeric
  result is reproduced from it.

The region-influence floor (silly_kicks.tracking.RunValuationParams) is a
silly-kicks parameter with no published provenance: it exists solely because
run_value is a maximum over a thresholded region. Its 0.1 default is a
spec-time starting value and is NOT calibrated -- ADR-042 records the
sensitivity probe (0.05 / 0.1 / 0.2) as its calibration, still to be run.
It is not a value carried over from either source above.

The cover shadow features in silly_kicks/tracking/_cover_shadows.py
(PR-S36, TF-30) implement methodologies described in:

- Cascioli, L., Wang, A., Stradiotti, L., Van Roy, M., Robberechts, P.,
  Wouters, M., Jaspers, A., & Davis, J. (2025). "Quantifying Off-Ball
  Defensive Impact through Cover Shadows." Hudl Research / DTAI, KU Leuven.
  (Lane Control physics-based pass-blocking model; blocking score
  counterfactual threat reduction metric)

- Spearman, W., Basye, A., Dick, G., Hotovy, R., & Pop, P. (2017).
  "Physics-Based Modeling of Pass Probabilities in Soccer." MIT Sloan SAC.
  (Ball drag model: quadratic air resistance with rho=1.22, C_D=0.25,
  A=0.038, m=0.42; referenced by Cascioli et al. for ball travel time)

The per-player influence primitives in silly_kicks/tracking/_player_influence.py
(PR-S51, TF-36 + TF-33) compose:

- Spearman, W. (2018). "Beyond Expected Goals." MIT Sloan Sports Analytics
  Conference.
  (pitch control decomposition, TTI kinematic model for uniquely reachable area)

- Singh, K. (2018). "Introducing Expected Threat (xT)." karun.in/blog/
  expected-threat
  (threat surface for per-player off-ball xT weighting)

The per-player composition (off-ball xT via PC share × xT, uniquely reachable
area generalized from GK-specific to all outfield) is novel to silly-kicks.

The ghost-GK positioning model in silly_kicks/tracking/_ghost_gk.py
(PR-S52, TF-18, GKDV Layer 2) implements methodologies described in:

- Le, H. M., Yue, Y., Carr, P. & Lucey, P. (2017). "Data-Driven Ghosting
  Using Deep Imitation Learning." MIT Sloan Sports Analytics Conference.
  (Ghost player positioning concept; league-average baselines)

- Dutta, R., Yurko, R. & Ventura, S. (2024). "NFL Ghosts: A framework for
  evaluating defender positioning with conditional density estimation."
  arXiv:2406.17220.
  (RFCDE density estimation for positional ghosts; leaf co-occurrence
  weighting; 2D KDE over tree-partitioned feature space)

- Pospisil, T. & Lee, A. B. (2018). "RFCDE: Random Forests for Conditional
  Density Estimation." arXiv:1804.05753.
  (Random forest leaf-assignment for CDE; weighted kernel density estimation)

- Hockney, R. W. & Eastwood, J. W. (1988). "Computer Simulation Using
  Particles." (Particle-mesh charge assignment: nearest-grid-point (NGP)
  and cloud-in-cell (CIC, bilinear) binning -- the kde_backend="fft"/"fft-cic"
  binning schemes for the O(k + m log m) FFT-convolution KDE; ADR-014)

The implementations are independent Python translations of the published
methodologies, not derived from any source code. Licensed under the same
terms as silly-kicks (MIT License).

The GKDV re-gate + physics-arms counterfactuals (the TF-19 cycle, ADR-037;
the silly_kicks/gkdv/ package itself ships in ADR-043) build on:

- Le, H. M., Yue, Y., Carr, P. & Lucey, P. (2017). "Data-Driven Ghosting Using
  Deep Imitation Learning." MIT Sloan Sports Analytics Conference.
  Data-Driven Ghosting. Used by: the GKDV ghost-substitution counterfactual
  engine (a league-average "ghost" goalkeeper substituted per frame to measure
  positioning-as-deterrent). Also cited above for the TF-18 ghost-GK positioning
  model; the TF-19 usage is the ghosting-counterfactual application.

- Kim, et al. (2025). "Better Prevent than Tackle: Valuing Defense in Soccer
  Based on Graph Neural Networks." arXiv:2512.10355. DEFCON-GNN.
  Composable comparator for the GKDV metric (per-defender EPV-reduction
  defensive-value credit; prevention-focused, not GK-positional). Comparator
  only -- NOT implemented in silly-kicks.

The two physics arms are counterfactual differences taken over published
methodologies rather than new ones, so each arm's underlying method is cited
here as well as at its own implementation site above:

- Bischofberger, J., & Baca, A. (2026). "Dangerous accessible space: a
  unified model of space and value in team sports." Journal of Big Data,
  13, 76. Package: accessible-space on PyPI (MIT).
  Used by: silly_kicks.gkdv.delta_das, which differences DAS across the
  factual and ghost-keeper frames via the narrow silly_kicks/gkdv/_das_port.py
  onto get_individual_das. Also cited above for the TF-28 DAS adapter
  (silly_kicks/tracking/_das.py); the TF-19 usage is the counterfactual
  application. DAS was explicitly designed to support hypotheticals.

- Spearman, W. (2018). "Beyond Expected Goals." MIT Sloan Sports Analytics
  Conference. (Off-ball scoring opportunity / pitch control.)
- Shaw, L., & Sudarshan, M. (2020). "A Framework for Tactical Analysis and
  Individual Offensive Production Assessment in Soccer Using Markov Models."
  (xT and pitch-control integration; source of lambda_gk = 3 * lambda_outfield.)
  Used by: silly_kicks.gkdv.delta_threat_suppression, which differences the
  threat-weighted pitch-control integral (silly_kicks.tracking.compute_threat_pc)
  across the factual and ghost-keeper frames. method="spearman" is a HARD API
  constraint in GkdvParams because lambda_gk exists only on SpearmanParams --
  the other pitch-control flavours are GK-blind, which would make the arm
  measure nothing. Both works are also cited above for TF-7 pitch control and
  TF-15 GK influence; the TF-19 usage is the counterfactual application.

